a visionary a revolutionary the son of a  nation for the nation not just lyrics to  his song 34 years after the  assassination of burkina faso's  president  he's still revered by millions of people  across french-speaking africa  1983 he was described as a feminist and  environmentalist than a 33 year old  soldier he was ahead of his time he  turned his back on international aid  money and loans from organizations like  the world bank instead favoring  self-sufficiency and calling for a more  equal relationship with france the  country's former colonial power on the  15th of october 1987 sankara was killed  by an armed group along with 12  officials his closest friend and former  colleague bless konpauer took power now  he and 13 others are due to go on trial  charged with murdering sankara this  trial is important for the manifestation  of the truth and that's why it's really  important that the blessed campaign be  honest one even if it's the only time in  your life  you must stand and tell the truth  bless  ousted in 2014 following a popular  uprising that brought an end to his 27  year rule he lives in exile in  neighboring ivory coast and has refused  to take part in the trial his supporters  see it as a political witch hunt  i don't understand that politicians that  once worked with him are now so bitter  and desperately seeking revenge  porkina is suffering enough already this  needs to stop  according to official government  documents sankara died a natural death  but in 2015 authorities exhumed his  remains from a grave an autopsy revealed  his body was riddled with bullets  in 2017 french president emmanuel macron  agreed to release secret files on  sankara's death that led to the opening  of the trial there is a desire for  justice not just in burkina faso but  throughout french-speaking west africa  because those behind  the assassination sought to  silence sankara in his ideals but it had  the reverse effect millions of people  across french-speaking west africa see  in sankara a symbol of resistance  against the powerful he's become a hero  and an icon celebrated in arts culture  and politics  many of those involved in the recent  cohen malian one in guinea last  september have referenced sankara's  leadership as an inspiration  these are some of his last words in  public  we need to accept we are africans it's  the only way to live with dignity  sankara says adding do you want to see  corrupt government officials or soldiers  then we will get rid of them this may  cost me my life but we are here to dare  shortly after this speech  sankara was shot dead nicholas hawk al  jazeera
 

